Two loads in one bank cost a cycle: the int8 GEMM has that collision and the bf16
GEMM does not
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie
22.0.0.2026090201). NO NPU SESSION was built and no hardware context was taken by this run;
it reads compiled ELFs and objects out of ~/.npu/cache and computes.

Commands (from the repo root, in the ironenv):
  python tools/aie_bank_check.py ~/.npu/cache/e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76 --kernel matmul_i8_i32
  python tools/aie_bank_check.py ~/.npu/cache/068c3151806dff6b4ab0f349 --kernel matmul_bf16_f32
  python tools/gemm_cost_model.py <int8 n=64 object> --measured-us 7458.1 \
      --bank-collision {none,loop,all}

PROVENANCE OF THE ENABLING MEASUREMENT. The cycle cost used here was NOT measured by this
session. It comes from a controlled experiment on branch `research/windows-lowlevel`
(`results/aie/memory_desktop2_20260909_m01_*.log`, kernel `kernels/memory_placement/`), which
holds the compiled function bytes identical and changes only the operand addresses, fitting a
length sweep at r-squared 1.0:

    two loads, same bank        12.0 core cycles per iteration
    two loads, separate banks   11.0
    one load,  same bank        11.0
    same bank, operand offset 64 / 128 / 256 bytes    12.0, 12.0, 12.0

So the second load is free across banks and costs exactly one cycle inside one bank, and the
granularity is the bank rather than the address. The same branch carries it through to a
single-core tiled GEMM at the same 64x64x64 panel geometry: 15,232 cycles per panel with the
operands in one bank against 14,208 with them separated, a difference of 1,024 cycles, which
is one cycle for each of that kernel's 1,024 inner iterations.

That branch and the driver work on local `main` are NOT merged into this one, so the log paths
above are named rather than linked, and the figures are quoted from them rather than re-run.

WHY THIS SESSION LOOKED. results/aie/gemm_cost_model_nest.log computes the int8 GEMM's issuing
cycles from its object code on the assumption that a hardware loop's bundle count is its cycle
count. A bank conflict is a counter-example to that assumption which owes nothing to locks,
streams or DMA -- the three exceptions that log already names. If the production GEMM has the
collision, its predicted cost is too low and its own 88.9% inner-loop figure is overstated.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. A CORRECTION TO THIS BRANCH'S OWN ISSUE-WIDTH ROW, WHICH IS WHY ANY OF THIS IS POSSIBLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
results/aie/aie2_isa_static.log and docs/SILICON.md 1.2 name the six VLIW slots
"b branch, a load, s store, x scalar, m move, v vector", read off the nop mnemonics. The
"branch" label is wrong. Tabulating every operation that appears in each slot of 226 STRICTLY
six-field bundles across the build caches -- six fields being the only encoding whose slot
identity is unambiguous -- gives:

  slot b: nopb x187, paddb x25, vldb x14
  slot a: nopa x165, mova x58, vlda x2, lda x1
  slot s: nops x167, vst x35, st x24
  slot x: nopx x76, add x51, event x23, lshl x22, ret x19, or x17, movx x10
  slot m: mov x90, nopm x82, add.nc x30, vbcst.16 x9, vbcst.32 x8, vmov x4, vshuffle x2
  slot v: nopv x221, vmac x4, vmac.f x1

Slot b is the SECOND LOAD UNIT, not the branch slot: `vldb` issues in it and `ret` issues in
the scalar slot x. Slots a and b each also carry an address-generation operation (`mova`,
`paddb`). The corrected reading is: two load units a and b, one store unit, one scalar unit
that also takes control flow, one move/broadcast unit, one vector unit.

This is not a footnote. It is the reason a bank conflict exists at all: a bundle naming both
a and b issues TWO loads in the same cycle, and that is the only situation in which two
addresses compete for one bank.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE BANK GEOMETRY, FROM THE COMPILER'S OWN TARGET MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
mlir-aie's include/aie/Dialect/AIE/IR/AIETargetModel.h, for the AIE2 device this machine is:
  getLocalMemorySize()  0x00010000   -> 64 KB of core-tile local data memory
  getNumBanks(col,row)  4 for a core tile (8 for a mem tile)
so a core tile's banks are 16 KB each. That is confirmed independently by the enabling
experiment above, whose MLIR labels addresses 16384 and 18432 both as `mem_bank = 1`.

Buffer addresses are NOT in the cached aie.mlir, which is pre-allocation and carries no
`address` attribute at all. They are in the linked core ELF's symbol table, which is what
tools/aie_bank_check.py reads. A core also addresses its neighbours' memories, so the symbol
table spans several 64 KB windows; the core's own window is the one containing
`_sp_start_value_DM_stack`.

