5. WHAT IT COSTS THE COST MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The same object, the same 4096x2048x2048 problem, with the paired-load penalty charged three
ways: not at all, only for the paired load inside the hardware loop (whose two operands are
the mmul tiles and so are known to be the colliding A and B), and for every paired-load bundle
in the function (an upper bound, since this tool does not resolve which buffers the ones
outside the loop read).

  --bank-collision none
  cycles per inner body      141  for 64 vmac -> 45.4% of one vmac per cycle
  paired-load bundles          1 in the hardware loop, 9 in the body around it
  ISSUING CYCLES PER CALL        2442
  MAC issue rate                107.3 MACs/cycle over the whole call, 41.9% of 256
  CORE ISSUING FRACTION          74.6 %   (pessimistic handoff: 117.5 %)
  not issuing                    25.4 %  (832 cycles per call)

  --bank-collision loop
  cycles per inner body      147  for 64 vmac -> 43.5% of one vmac per cycle
  paired-load bundles          1 in the hardware loop, 9 in the body around it
  ISSUING CYCLES PER CALL        2538
  MAC issue rate                103.3 MACs/cycle over the whole call, 40.3% of 256
  CORE ISSUING FRACTION          77.5 %   (pessimistic handoff: 120.4 %)
  not issuing                    22.5 %  (736 cycles per call)

  --bank-collision all
  cycles per inner body      156  for 64 vmac -> 41.0% of one vmac per cycle
  paired-load bundles          1 in the hardware loop, 9 in the body around it
  ISSUING CYCLES PER CALL        2682
  MAC issue rate                 97.7 MACs/cycle over the whole call, 38.2% of 256
  CORE ISSUING FRACTION          81.9 %   (pessimistic handoff: 124.8 %)
  not issuing                    18.1 %  (592 cycles per call)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE INT8 GEMM HAS THE COLLISION AND THE BF16 GEMM DOES NOT, AND THE REASON IS INVERTED FROM
WHAT YOU WOULD GUESS. In the int8 build the output tile C takes banks 0 and 1 and BOTH input
tiles land in bank 2, with bank 3 left completely empty. In the bf16 build the same allocator
puts C in bank 0, A in bank 1 and B in bank 2, and bank 3 is again empty. The difference is
that int8's operand tiles are half the size of bf16's, so the pair fits inside one 16 KB bank
and the allocator packs them there. The int8 kernel is penalised BECAUSE its data is smaller.

THE EXPOSURE IS WORSE THAN THE COUNT SUGGESTS. Both kernels have exactly one paired-load
bundle in their steady-state loop. The bf16 loop is 32 bundles, so one extra cycle would be
3.1%. The int8 loop is 9, so it is 11.1%. The shorter, denser loop is the more exposed one.

WHAT IT DOES TO THE MODEL. Charging the loop's paired load raises the int8 GEMM's issuing cost
per call from 2,442 to 2,538 cycles and its issuing fraction from 74.6% to 77.5%; charging
every paired-load bundle raises them to 2,682 and 81.9%. So the unexplained residual, which
this branch has been calling starvation, narrows from 25.4% to between 18% and 23%. The MAC
rate over a whole call falls slightly, from 107.3 to 103.3 MACs per cycle.

AND THE FIX IS FREE, WHICH MAKES IT A CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT. Bank 3 is empty in both builds.
Moving one input tile into it changes the core's issuing time by a known amount and changes
NOTHING about data movement: same bytes, same DMA, same ObjectFifo depth, same schedule, same
function bytes. That is exactly the lever needed to test the per-buffer floor that
results/aie/gemm_cost_model_nest.log proposes.

  H12. Placing A and B in different banks on the whole_array int8 design will NOT change the
       measured NPU time, because the core is not the critical path -- the per-buffer floor
       is. Fails if the dispatch speeds up by anything like the ~4% the issuing-cost change
       predicts, which would mean the design is issue-bound after all and the floor is not
       what limits it.

  H12 is worth running precisely because both outcomes are informative, and it is cheap: one
  rebuild with a buffer address or bank override, one dispatch, no new instrument.

WHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE EARLIER LAW. docs/SILICON.md 1.2 says a hardware loop's cycles equal
its bundle count, with locks, streams and DMA named as the exceptions. There is a fourth, and
it is invisible to the trace unit's stall taxonomy in the sense that it is not a lock, stream
or cascade stall: a paired load whose two addresses share a bank. The law should be read as
holding for a loop whose simultaneous loads are in different banks.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. A TEMPTING JOIN WITH THE DRIVER WORK, TESTED AND FALSIFIED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Local `main` carries a driver characterisation (`results/aie/windows_context_switch_bench.log`)
measuring an NPU hardware-context-switch penalty of +747.75 us: same-context dispatch averages
120.25 us, alternating between two contexts averages 867.99 us, a 7.22x slowdown. It also finds
an exact five-context ceiling in amdxe.sys matching Phoenix's five physical columns, with a
sixth rejected as NTSTATUS 0xc01e0009.

This repo's largest unexplained blocker is the two-process handoff floor that erased all 33 of
the bf16 GroupNorm's node wins, whose smallest shape measures 789.8 us per call
(results/aie/groupnorm_bf16_handoff_floor_npu.log). 789.8 against 747.75 is close enough to be
worth testing, and the test refutes it. Fitting that log's whole table against element count:

  slope      78.4005 ns per element
  intercept  147.2 us
  r2         0.999725

  L          measured us   fit us    us per element
     9408        789.8      884.8      0.08395
    18816       1505.3     1622.4      0.08000
    37632       3090.0     3097.6      0.08211
    75264       6179.8     6047.9      0.08211
   150528      12167.7    11948.7      0.08083
   301056      23618.9    23750.1      0.07845

The floor is 99.97% a per-element cost. Its fixed component is 147.2 us, a fifth of the
context-switch penalty, and the agreement at the smallest shape is a coincidence of that one
row. The original log's own diagnosis stands: the floor is conversion-bound, bf16 pack and
unpack accounting for ~90% of the round trip at the largest shape. Nobody should write that
the handoff floor is the context switch.

WHAT THE DRIVER WORK DOES SETTLE FOR THIS BRANCH. Its userspace dispatch-preparation floor is
8.76 us and its hardware runlist batching overhead is 3.39 us per run. The int8 GEMM measured
here is ONE dispatch containing 65,536 kernel calls, so those costs are paid once over 7,458 us
and cannot be the per-call residual. That eliminates the host and the driver as candidates for
the 18-23% and leaves on-chip data movement, which is what H9 predicts and what a stream-port
trace would confirm.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- NOTHING HERE WAS MEASURED ON HARDWARE BY THIS SESSION. The 12-vs-11 cycle cost, the
  15,232-vs-14,208 panel slopes, the driver floors and the context-switch penalty are all
  quoted from logs on two other branches. The contribution of this run is the static survey,
  the slot correction, and the cost-model arithmetic.
- The collision is a HAZARD, not a measured cost, for these two kernels. This tool does not
  resolve which buffers a given paired load reads. For the hardware loop the operands are the
  mmul input tiles, so the collision is certain there; for the nine paired loads outside the
  loop it is an upper bound.
- Whether the +1 cycle composes the same way in a kernel with several paired loads per
  iteration is untested. The enabling experiment had exactly one.
- The bank map is read from the first of 16 core ELFs in each build. Allocation is per core
  and the others were not checked.
- One power mode, one machine, two kernels. The conv kernels have no surviving build cache, so
  the most-lost op class in this repo was NOT surveyed and should be when one is rebuilt.
